==Trivia==
*His Japanese name, Manborg Suzuki, is derived from 翻車魚 ''manbō'' (sunfish), ''cyborg'' (likely referencing his ability to fire missiles) and 鈴木 ''Suzuki'' (a common Japanese surname using the kanji for "bell" and "tree").
**The combination of "borg" and "Suzuki" may also be a reference to Ichirō Suzuki, the ''Gundam''-obsessed schoolteacher from the manga ''[[wikipedia:Cyborg Kuro-chan|Cyborg Kuro-chan]]''. In the anime adaptation of ''Cyborg Kuro-chan'', Suzuki has a lot of connections with aliens, which may tie into [[Spacefish Jones]].
**Although the Japanese names of Sunfish Jones, Spacefish Jones and [[Jumbo Jones]] all have parts that can be taken as "borg", they aren't all the same: Sunfish Jones and Jumbo Jones have ボーグ ''bōgu'', while Spacefish Jones has ボルグ ''borugu''.
*Sunfish Jones is quite literally a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pterophyllum Angel Fish].
*His English description references the Northrop Grumman [[wikipedia:Northrop Grumman B-2 Spirit|B-2 Spirit]] stealth bomber. Indeed, stealth is a recurring theme in his variants' descriptions as well:
**Spacefish Jones is an extremely slim bioweapon who is nearly invisible when seen from the front, but is easily detected when viewed from the sides.
**Golem Sunfish was at one point sealed in the ocean's depths, but had subtly resurfaced after some time.
**Jumbo Jones came to be after some genetic engineering in the [[Laboratory of Relics]], which resulted in him losing his stealth capabilities in favor of raw strength.
*There used to be an obvious typo in Sunfish Jones' English description, which said "shaped like '''an''' stealth bomber".
*Despite firing multiple missiles in his attack animation, Sunfish Jones only hits once per attack, when the first missile lands at 59f.

